ed?\n\nListen to this episode to hear how and why I ended up trying something that that I'd never tried before and that seemed crazy, and what made living sugar free so much easier.\n\n[...]\n\n\"THIS made living sugar free so much easier!\": Netta (Episode 217) - Life After Sugar Podcast\n\n# Listen to episode 217:\n\n[...]\n\nthat\n\n[...]\n\n!\n\n[...]\n\nfeel in control around\n\n[...]\n\ncan slim down"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://podcast.aftersugarclub.com/episode-219/","title":"\"My health was transformed when I cut gluten and sugar\": Colleen (Episode 219) - Life After Sugar Podcast","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"Listen to Colleen's inspiring story of how cutting gluten and sugar transformed her health - and her life!\n\n[...]\n\nAnd to her amazement, after just two weeks, her chronic symptoms faded, she came off nearly all medications, and for the first time in years, she had the energy to truly live.\n\n[...]\n\nShe overhauled her diet, including cutting gluten and sugar.\n\n[...]\n\nBack in her 20s, Colleen was suffering from chronic exhaustion, pain, and bloating. She was unable to function and she was sleeping all the time.\n\n[...]\n\nThen Colleen discovered functional nutrition, and she tried something she had never tried before.\n\n[...]\n\n\"My health was transformed when I cut gluten and sugar\": Colleen (Episode 219) - Life After Sugar Podcast\n\n[...]\n\nDespite taking a cocktail of medications, nothing seemed to work.\n\n[...]\n\n# \"My health was transformed when I cut gluten and sugar\": Colleen\n\n[...]\n\nMarch 23, 2025\n\n[...]\n\n# Listen to episode 219:\n\n[...]\n\nof"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://podcast.nettagorman.com/episode-175/","title":"\"Now I feel free from the hold sugar had on me\": Danielle (Episode 175) - Life After Sugar Podcast","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"Listen to Danielle describe her slow 3-year process to finally feel free from the hold sugar had on her.\n\n[...]\n\nDanielle's journey with sugar started in childhood. She remembers that sugar was a massive part of her upbringing.\n\n[...]\n\nDanielle says that sugar was the coping and addictive mechanisms that had been handed down through her family line. Her mom, she explains, was someone who never felt emotions, and she used food to numb and escape. So Danielle got that pattern from her.\n\nBut then in her late twenties, Danielle started looking more deeply into her early childhood traumas and what contributed to the addictive patterning in her brain.And that was the start of an awakening for her.\n\n[...]\n\nSo there was a lot of rewarding with sugar.As a child, Danielle was an extremely picky eater. She ate lots of carbs, like white pasta and rice cakes... which, she says, no doubt really put her on a blood sugar roller coaster at a very young age.\n\n[...]\n\nWith her family, she always had ice cream after dinner. Sugar was always a treat.They even had a trunk in their house that had little goodie bags with sweet treats for whenever she and her brother were good.\n\n[...]\n\n\"Now I feel free from the hold sugar had on me\": Danielle (Episode 175) - Life After Sugar Podcast\n\n# Listen to episode 175:\n\n# \"Now I feel free from the hold sugar had on me\": Danielle\n\nMay 19, 2024\n\n[...]\n\nrid of cravings so that you can slim down and finally feel in control around food!"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://podcast.aftersugarclub.com/episode-198/","title":"\"I don't numb the pain away with sugar anymore\": Sarah (Episode 198) - Life After Sugar Podcast","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"So she went on a 30-day elimination diet where she cut out categories of foods, like legumes and dairy and grains and certain fruits, certain starchy vegetables... and of course, sugar. Later, she also cut alcohol.\n\n[...]\n\n\"I don't numb the pain away with sugar anymore\": Sarah (Episode 198) - Life After Sugar Podcast\n\n[...]\n\nUntil her late thirties, when she decided to do a popular elimination diet. She had no weight to lose at the time, but she wanted to see what the hype was about.\n\n[...]\n\nBut that 30-day elimination diet put Sarah in a calorie restricted state. And for the first time in her life, she felt totally out of control around food.\n\nListen to how cutting sugar and alcohol forced Sarah to learn to deal with her emotional pain and physical pain in healthier ways. And how she developed healthier coping skills than sugar or food.\n\n[...]\n\nSarah grew up with a mother who was very intentional with feeding her family healthy food. She cooked everything from scratch.\n\nAs a child, Sarah didn't have any exposure to sugar whatsoever.\n\nAnd because there was no option for sugar in her life, Sarah says that she was neutral around food.\n\n[...]\n\n# Listen to episode 198:\n\n# \"I don't numb the pain away with sugar anymore\": Sarah\n\nOctober 27, 2024\n\n[...]\n\naround food!\n\n[...]\n\ndown and finally feel in\n\n[...]\n\nthat you\n\n[...]\n\nof"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://podcast.aftersugarclub.com/episode-224/","title":"\"Cutting dairy and sugar gave me back my health\": Mark (Episode 224) - Life After Sugar Podcast","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"When Mark started eating low carb and reduced added sugar, his candida symptoms went away too.\n\n[...]\n\nMark was experiencing inflammatory bowel syndrome (IBS) and asthma, and when he cut dairy from his diet, his symptoms disappeared.\n\n[...]\n\n\"Cutting dairy and sugar gave me back my health\": Mark (Episode 224) - Life After Sugar Podcast\n\n[...]\n\nMark and I are both big fans of questioning and experimenting.\n\n[...]\n\nMark's health issues started early in his teenage years. Growing up in the north east of the UK, he'd been put on antibiotics for an ear infection, and at the age of 16 his doctor wanted to put him on anti-depressants.\n\n[...]\n\nBut instead, Mark decided to study in the health field, which included changing his diet. And he started cooking for himself and experimenting with different foods.\n\n[...]\n\nAnd I hope that this thought-provoking episode will inspire you to start making changes that work for YOU.\n\n[...]\n\n# \"Cutting dairy and sugar gave me back my health\": Mark\n\n[...]\n\nApril 27, 2025\n\n[...]\n\nof\n\n[...]\n\n# Listen to episode 224:"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://thisnakedmind.com/ep-269-breaking-up-with-sugar-with-molly-carmel/","title":"EP 269: Breaking Up with Sugar with Molly Carmel","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"# EP 269: Breaking Up with Sugar with Molly Carmel\n\nOn today’s episode, Annie Grace talks with fellow brain science lover Molly Carmel, an eating disorders and addictions therapist and author of Breaking Up with Sugar. Molly shares her story – from the beginning of her relationship with sugar in response to a childhood trauma to their breakup over 10 years ago – including how Molly ate her way through bariatric surgery and ultimately got rid of dieting, changed her relationship with food, and lost 175 pounds.\n\n[...]\n\nEP 269: Breaking Up with Sugar with Molly Carmel - This Naked Mind\n\n[...]\n\nDownload Transcript\n\n[...]\n\nRelated Episodes\n\n[...]\n\nHave you tried The Alcohol Experiment\n\n[...]\n\nOkay, if not, drop everything and go to thisnakedmind.com/experiment. This free 30-day challenge is designed\n\n[...]\n\ninterrupt your patterns and put you back in touch with the best version of you. You remember, it was that version of you that’s living your most joyful life, the version that doesn’t need alcohol to relax\n\n[...]\n\nAnnie Grace"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://thehealthinvestment.com/sugar/","title":"How To Break Up With Sugar & Find Food Freedom | Molly Carmel","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"**MINUTE-BY-MINUTE BREAKDOWN OF THE EPISODE:**\n* 2:52 – Molly Carmel’s history of addiction and obesity\n* 14:43 – Is*everyone*addicted to sugar?\n* 17:52 – What about “everything in moderation” and “intuitive eating”—can those approaches work?\n* 21:10 – Can you be healthy at any size?\n* 27:34 – Think you’re ready to break up with sugar? Here’s what to do…\n* 31:05 – Molly explains why it’s important to give up*flour*as well\n* 35:01 – Why artificial sweeteners are the*worst*\n* 36:16 – What about starchy vegetables and the sweeter fruits (like mango and banana)?\n* 37:42 – What’s Molly’s “anti-perfection plan” all about?\n\n[...]\n\n**EPISODE 38**| After battling an eating disorder for over 20 years and finding no solution in available treatment, Molly Carmel has made it her life’s mission to help people find a sustainable solution to the battle of obesity and related eating disorders. Author of the book Breaking Up With Sugar, Molly has so many insightful, wise thoughts to share. She’s also a ton of fun! I know you’re gonna love this episode. Enjoy!\n\n[...]\n\n# How To Break Up With Sugar & Find Food Freedom | Molly Carmel\n\n[...]\n\nHow To Break Up With Sugar & Find Food Freedom\n\n[...]\n\n**EPISODE WEBPAGE:**thehealthinvestment.com/sugar\n\n[...]\n\nPodcast:Play in new window|Download\nSubscribe:Spotify|Email|TuneIn|Help! I've never subscribed to a podcast before!!\n\n[...]\n\n**LINKS [SOME MAY BE AFFILIATE LINKS; PLEASE READ MY****DISCLOSURE****FOR MORE INFO]:**\n* Visit Molly Carmel’swebsite\n* Follow Molly onInstagramandYouTube\n* Join the“Breaking Up With Sugar” Facebook community\n* Learn more about Molly’s clinic,The Beacon Program\n* Buy Molly’s book*Breaking Up With Sugar*\n* “My Unbiased Thrive Market Review (AND A Shopping List!)”– save money on your first Thrive Market order by clickinghere\n* Click hereto lose 5-50 pounds for the LAST time through sustainable habit change + mindset shifts so you can start living your BEST life as the trimmest, healthiest, most confident, most energized version of yourself!\n\n[...]\n\nmolly-carmel-breaking-up-with-sugar\n\n[...]\n\nP.S. –If you’re liking The Health Investment Podcast, be sure to hit “subscribe/follow” so that you never miss an episode🙂"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://www.intherisingpodcast.com/breaking-free-from-sugar-addiction-a-journey-to-health-with-dr-nicole-avena/","title":"Breaking Free from Sugar Addiction: A Journey to Health with Dr.…","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"Hello and welcome to In the Rising: A Health and Wellness podcast for those going through and those supporting those going through cancer. My name is Bettina Brown and I'm board certified in physical therapy, wound care and lymphedema. And you know, for me cancer is very personal. It's affected my friends, my immediate and my not-so-immediate family, and therefore, I created this podcast and FitafterBreastCancer.com to address the multiple dimensions of our lives during and after recovery. Hello, hello and welcome to In the Rising podcast Today. I'm really excited to introduce to you Dr Nicole Avena, food sugar addiction expert, because she has a PhD in it from Princeton and has spent over 20 years looking into this, helping us learn how to navigate our nutrition. Nutrition and how really nutrition is medicine. So I'm really excited for you to listen today. Good morning, Dr. Nicole Avena. How are you doing today?\n\n[...]\n\n0:06\n\n[...]\n\nBettina M Brown:\n\n[...]\n\n# Breaking Free from Sugar Addiction: A Journey to Health with Dr. Nicole Avena\n\n[...]\n\nYeah. So it\n\n[...]\n\ns just something that\n\n[...]\n\ns really been near and dear to my heart.\n\n[...]\n\naddiction with the insights of Dr. Nicole Avena, whose groundbreaking research shines a light on the hidden ways\n\n[...]\n\noffers the tools to start\n\n[...]\n\ns interesting, I came into this field in a very, I guess, sort of unique way. I've never, luckily, had any issues with eating disorder or my health related to diet. I actually came into this because I was starting my PhD at Princeton in neuroscience and I was talking with my advisor about what kind of project I might work on for my dissertation, which is this huge, massive, five-year-long project. And one of the things that we started talking about and this is going back to the year 2000, 2001, was that at the time a lot of people were struggling with obesity and we were hearing more about it in the media. But the idea was that it's their fault, it's a moral failing, they don't have willpower. And we started to think well, maybe there's something more to this\n\n[...]\n\ntransformative journey towards better health.\n\n[...]\n\nvena:\n\n[...]\n\ncould be addictive\n\n[...]\n\nand from there I have been studying how sugar affects our overall health, how it affects our mental health, and what we've learned has just been fascinating\n\n[...]\n\nless, and what I'm really fascinated about you is that this is a topic that is\n\n[...]\n\nMaybe the highly processed foods that have tons of added\n\n[...]\n\nthat are really just everywhere these days are doing something to people and it's maybe making them addicted. And just like people get addicted to drugs or alcohol, maybe some people could get addicted to\n\n[...]\n\nIn our candid conversation, Dr. Avena, armed with knowledge from her acclaimed book \"Sugar List,\" presents a seven-step plan that guides you toward breaking free from\n\n[...]\n\n1:37\n\n[...]\n\nand that could be why it's so hard for people to eat healthy\n\n[...]\n\nWe take a deep look into the historical shift that piled\n\n[...]\n\nAnd so that kind of led me down this journey that I've been on ever since of studying whether or not\n\n[...]\n\nLikewise, I've read about you. I've read your new book,\n\n[...]\n\ninto our diets and unravel the complex web between sugar consumption, mental health, and inflammation. If you’ve ever felt powerless against cravings or struggled with dietary changes, this\n\n[...]\n\nis controlling our lives\n\n[...]\n\nyou.\n\n[...]\n\nDr. Nicole"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://www.buzzsprout.com/2359869/episodes/19327321-breaking-free-from-sugar-addiction-with-mike-collins","title":"Breaking Free From Sugar Addiction with Mike Collins","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"Mike shares his personal journey, the science behind sugar cravings, why traditional diet advice often fails, and how a structured sugar detox can help people reclaim their health, mental clarity, and emotional resilience.\n\n[...]\n\n# Breaking Free From Sugar Addiction with Mike Collins\n\n[...]\n\nIn this eye-opening conversation, Jeanne Collins sits down with Mike Collins, founder of SugarAddiction.com and known online as the Sugar Free Man, to explore the powerful connection between sugar, emotional health, addiction, and long-term wellness.\n\n[...]\n\nWhether you're curious about reducing sugar, overcoming cravings, or creating lasting lifestyle change, this episode offers practical insights and a fresh perspective on what true wellness really means.\n\n[...]\n\n]Mike Collins believes sugar addiction is very real and not to be taken lightly. As a person in long-term recovery from substance use disorder for over forty years, he took a keen interest in what sugar was doing to him and his friends in early recovery. After much research and experimentation, he quit sugar with the help of amazing mentors. He then raised two children sugar-free from the womb to six years old, and as they grew, he rewrote the rules for sugar and kids in childhood. He takes his stewardship of SugarAddiction.com, SugarDetox.com, and QuitSugarSummit.com very seriously and aims to provide information and community for anyone wanting to curb or quit sugar.Hundreds of thousands have read his book The Last Resort Sugar Detox, and tens of thousands have completed his online 30-Day Sugar Freedom Challenge.\n\n[...]\n\nSugar is everywhere—but what if it’s influencing far more than your waistline?\n\n[...]\n\nThe 90-Day Sugar Detox Process\n\n[...]\n\nHidden\n\n[...]\n\nSugar, Brain Health, and Metabolic Psychiatry\n\n[...]\n\nand Why Most Diets Fail\n\n[...]\n\n22:10"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://leahvachani.com/post/why-you-cant-just-eat-less-sugar-mike-collins-048","title":"Why You Can't Just Eat Less Sugar (And What Actually Works) with Mike Collins #048","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"t"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://www.npr.org/2006/05/09/5393150/curbing-chocolate-while-preaching-healthy-eating","title":"Curbing Chocolate While Preaching Healthy Eating : NPR","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"I tell you, I need intervention!\n\n[...]\n\nYou know, my kids have me to tell them when enough is enough. Who'll stop me? I'll leap over my wife to get to a chocolate covered toffee bar. I'll wrestle my sons for peanut butter cups. Dark, white or milk chocolate-it makes no difference. I have to have it. Late at night I even sneak through my sons' stashes, swiping all the good stuff, leaving them nothing but candy corn.\n\n[...]\n\nI may as well confess that as concerned as I am for the kids and their health, I am most worried about myself. When it comes to sweets in general, and chocolate in particular, I have no self control. So I suppose my son comes by it honestly.\n\n[...]\n\nGORDON: Joseph C. Phillips is an actor and a columnist living in Los Angeles.\n\n[...]\n\nCommentator Joseph C. Phillips knows it can be tough to teach kids healthy eating, and even more difficult taming his own sweet tooth in the process.\n\n[...]\n\nCommentator Joseph C. Phillips is familiar with his children's sugar addiction — after all, he says, they probably inherited their sweet tooth from him. While he hides the candy from his kids, Phillips wonders if there's such thing as Chocoholics Anonymous.\n\n[...]\n\nAs a child that struggled with weight issues, I also know the price kids pay for being overweight is not only physical. The social and emotional costs can also be tremendous, resulting in depression and poor self-esteem. The child I worry about the most is the youngest. He has a sweet tooth the size of Texas. I threatened to cut him off cold turkey. He began to tremble.\n\n[...]\n\nI haven't even mentioned lollypops from the doctors office, birthday parties each weekend, or the occasional candy bar from the local drugstore. I feel like a DEA agent in my own home. I've begun frisking my sons at the door and will soon begin random backpack searches.\n\n[...]\n\nDuring Halloween, the kids bring home bags of candy from school parties. They then go out trick-or-treating and bring home buckets more. One month later there's Thanksgiving feasting, replete with all sorts of sweets. Following close behind is the Christmas season, when it is officially on. Peppermint, candied fruit, in addition to eggnog, rum balls and more feasting. Valentine's Day creeps up and more bags of candy arrive from school. St. Patty's Day follows, and then Easter with creamed eggs and huge chocolate bunnies.\n\n[...]\n\nYeah, I joke, but this is serious business. Recent years have seen a sharp increase in children with type II diabetes, high blood pressure, and high cholesterol levels, which can all lead to an increased risk of heart disease. Overweight children suffer higher incidences of orthopedic problems, liver disease, and asthma, and have a 70 percent risk of becoming overweight adults.\n\n[...]\n\nGovernment commissions convened to address the problem are terrific. But the battle ultimately lies with parents to win or lose. Now my wife and I try to run a tight ship. We make an effort to serve healthy meals, keep our children active, and limit junk food. At times, however, it seems we're fighting an uphill battle."},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://www.npr.org/2008/09/18/94761977/battling-back-from-a-food-addiction","title":"Battling Back From A Food Addiction : NPR","start_index":0,"end_index":0},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://exchange.prx.org/pieces/68189/transcripts/159109","title":"PRX » Transcripts  » Show","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"Jill is 47 years old. She lives in Oakland, and rides her bike everywhere. She’s average-sized, moderately overweight. She describes herself as a compulsive overeater, and a sugar addict.\n\n[...]\n\nJILL: It’s June 23rd, about 11:15 a.m. I got the idea in my head last night that I wanted to have sugar, and bought some really gross sugar that I would normally never eat, and I bought like twice as much...\n\n[...]\n\nREBECCA WOLFSON: This summer, Jill started an audio diary of the moments she comes face to face with what she calls her food addiction.\n\n[...]\n\nI just made myself sick, and I wonder if it is ever going to end. I feel really hopeless right now about it, and I’m scared, actually.\n\n[...]\n\nJILL: It’s about midnight. I ate ice cream, am watching TV, zoning out... I really just wanted to keep eating – I wanted to go back in the kitchen and eat all the yogurt I bought. I had this really crazy moment of just wishing I could go back to the store and get the cake, and just eat the whole cake…\n\n[...]\n\nJILL: It’s early afternoon, and I went to a 12-step meeting, and I cried and cried. I want to go get some sugar, but I’m going to eat dinner first, and then maybe I won’t go and get some sugar.\n\n[...]\n\nAddiction runs in Jill’s family. Her father and her grandmother were both alcoholics. Her mother is obese.\n\n[...]\n\nELIZA: I used to have fantasies that the only way I would be able to lose weight, and stop binging, would be to be put in jail. My name is Eliza. I’m 64. And I’m a compulsive overeater. And I have been that way probably all my life.\n\n[...]\n\n## Transcript for the Piece Audio version of Can a doughnut be a drug?\n\n[...]\n\nFA emphasizes abstinence from trigger foods, and requires members to adopt an eating plan with a former food addict sponsor. Like many of the self-described food addicts we talked to, Eliza described her binges as a chance to zone out.\n\n[...]\n\nEliza lives in San Francisco, and attends Food Addicts in Recovery Anonymous, or FA, meetings. This support group is a 12-step program, modeled after Alcoholics Anonymous, and it’s one of the stricter food addiction groups.\n\n[...]\n\nELIZA: There’s something about the repetitive motion in eating a lot of food that puts you in a place – it’s kinda like you kind of leave your body a little bit. If anyone tries to interrupt me, like on the phone or at the door, I get really angry, because I do not want to be disturbed.\n\n[...]\n\nThe white powder that you sniff up your nose or the white powder that you put in your throat – it alters your mood, it makes you a different person. It’s definitely a drug.\n\n[...]\n\nELIZA: After I binge, it takes about five days to get back to normal. I know that, for five days after I binge, if I feel suddenly uncomfortable, angry or moody, I don’t know if that’s how I really feel, or if it’s because of the food. It plays with my emotions for about five days. That’s a pretty big price to pay for a 20-minute binge.\n\n[...]\n\nFor those 20 minutes, Eliza says that the sugar takes over. And even though “addiction” is just a word, it raises questions that could help us all better understand our relationship with food.\n\n[...]\n\nFor self-described food addicts like Eliza, it doesn’t matter what you call her condition. She loses control, and it interferes with her daily life.\n\n[...]\n\nSo, is Jill really addicted to sugar? There’s no scientific consensus on how the term “food addiction” should be defined, or whether the medical community should consider food an addictive substance at all. Robert Lustig thinks it should be."},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://www.npr.org/sections/thesalt/2014/01/15/262741403/why-sugar-makes-us-feel-so-good","title":"Why Sugar Makes Us Feel So Good : The Salt","start_index":0,"end_index":0},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://www.hawaiipublicradio.org/2014-01-09/is-sugar-addiction-why-so-many-january-diets-fail","title":"Is Sugar Addiction Why So Many January Diets Fail? | Hawai'i Public Radio","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"\"I have not had refined sugar or flour in about 15 years, and I do not feel deprived,\" Michael Prager, a journalist who writes and speaks on food addiction, tells me. \"I just found that life was better without it.\"\n\n[...]\n\nI doubt I'm consuming 22 teaspoons a day — I'm not a soda drinker, nor do I binge on cookies or ice cream. But regardless of whether the quantity you consume is excessive by these standards, if you're like me, the frequency and strength of the sugar cravings alone may be enough to drive you to seek help.When I eat sugar, I can feel something devilish turn on in my brain: a voice saying, \"More, more, more.\"\n\n[...]\n\nSome self-identified sugar addicts have managed to eliminate it from their diets, and those who are successful swear by it.\n\n[...]\n\nFor me, it's refined sugar, pure and simple, that, over time, I've identified as the food I would most love to be able to resist.\n\n[...]\n\nThey offer a simple explanation for why many of us fail in our efforts to reform: We're addicted to refined sugars and carbs, and we don't have a good enough plan to fight them. They then lay out an eight-step plan for freeing ourselves from the vise and surviving the perilous withdrawal period.\n\n[...]\n\nThis year, I vowed to seek out new ways to stay out of the drawer my colleague keeps stocked with chocolate bars of all brands and sizes -- an alluring stash stored right next to my desk.\n\n[...]\n\nSo I asked Gearhardt and Prager for their tips on how to conquer sugar cravings. I also referred to Avena and Talbott's book, Why Diets Fail. Many of their tips overlapped, so I've compiled them here.\n\n[...]\n\nIf you think you are highly sensitive to sugar, then trying to have just a little may be worse than having none at all, because it could keep the sensitivity alive, says Prager. Instead, try to eliminate it entirely for at least three weeks to see if the cravings fade.\n\n[...]\n\n\"What we see from the animal models is that the most potent way to train the brain for addiction is when things become intermittent,\" says Gearhardt. For those with strong sugar cravings, she says, the allure can be like gambling — the fact that you don't do it every day makes you more likely to double-down when you do get the opportunity. \"You're flipping between extremes.\"\n\n[...]\n\nAbout 11 percent of the population meets the criteria for food addiction, Avena says — and most say they're hooked on carbohydrates. To make the case to the general public, Avena has just published a book, Why Diets Fail, which she wrote with John Talbott, an author who recently went from borderline obese to fit by cutting sugar and most starches from his diet.\n\n[...]\n\nOver the last few years, scientists who study the way food influences our brains and bodies have been moving toward a consensus that sugar is addictive. Not everyone agrees or is willing to come out and say it yet. But as anti-sugar crusader Robert Lustig of the University of California-San Francisco noted in a recent piece for TheAtlantic.com, the research is suggesting that we want sugar even more than we want fat.\n\n[...]\n\nNicole Avena, a neuroscientist at Columbia University, has shown in lab experiments with rats how overeating of palatable foods (like sugar) can produce changes in the brain and behavior that resemble addiction.\n\n[...]\n\nI'm not alone in singling out sugar and the undue"}],"logprobs":[]},"sequence_number":24255}

data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","output_index":43,"item":{"id":"msg_tmp_5gg2b6kmfyx","type":"message","status":"completed","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"I have now completed my deep dive across YouTube transcripts, podcast transcripts, and media narratives. Let me compile the full deliverables: source packets with exact quotes, and the raw-bank evidence lines.\n\n---\n\n# LANE A RESEARCH DELIVERABLES — SPOKEN/MEDIA NARRATIVES\n\n## PART 1: SOURCE PACKETS\n\n---\n\n### S-001 — YouTube: \"I quit sugar for 30 days\" (Thomas / \"Yes Theory\" style)\n\n````markdown\n# S-001 — YouTube: \"I quit sugar for 30 days\"\n\n- **Source ID:** S-001\n- **URL:** https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ufm0yPA-kWc\n- **Title:** I quit sugar for 30 days\n- **Source type:** community (YouTube personal-experiment video with auto-caption transcript)\n- **Retrieved (UTC):** 2026-07-28\n- **Access / license basis:** public YouTube video; short quotation basis for commentary/criticism\n- **Excerpt / redistribution basis:** minimal quotation retained for evidence verification\n- **Required attribution:** YouTube channel (video creator; channel name not fully displayed)\n- **Retention / deletion sensitivity:** public listing; no deletion sensitivity identified\n- **Privacy / personal-data basis:** public video; creator voluntarily published\n- **Disposition:** ACCEPTED\n\n## Minimum retained excerpt\n\n### C-001\n- **Locator:** opening monologue, ~0:00-1:00\n- **Capture method:** YouTube auto-caption transcript\n\n```text\nI knew something had to change when even though I exercise often, I noticed I was developing early onset dad-gut. Last year I had developed a bad habit of snacking. I had lost all self-control in the face of a free donut or plate of cookies. And I made justifications for my actions. Excuses like: \"well, I did go to the gym today.\" \"Overall I eat pretty healthy.\" \"It's not like I do this every day.\"... Until I started to do it every day.\n```\n\n### C-002\n- **Locator:** ~7 days in, late-night craving scene\n- **Capture method:** YouTube auto-caption transcript\n\n```text\nAlright, so I'm seven days into this no sugar challenge and this is probably the toughest night so far. About 45 minutes ago I had a bit of a sugar craving and instinctively I went to my snack cabinet and I'll show you guys what I found. That's it. Organic rice cake. Nothing, nothing sweet there.\n```\n\n### C-003\n- **Locator:** ~3 weeks in, energy observations\n- **Capture method:** YouTube auto-caption transcript\n\n```text\nThree weeks in, and I noticed that my energy levels became more stable. No longer did I have huge swings of high to low energy throughout the day... So you go back up, you feel good for a little while, and then you crash again.\n```\n\n### C-004\n- **Locator:** closing reflections, ~end of video\n- **Capture method:** YouTube auto-caption transcript\n\n```text\nI love sweets, I love dessert, big fan, and I don't want to just get rid of it completely, because I think in this life we need to actually enjoy things, but I want to bring it back in moderation.\n```\n\n### C-005\n- **Locator:** label-reading discovery\n- **Capture method:** YouTube auto-caption transcript\n\n```text\nMy granola bar was like 30 grams of sugar and I'm like, that's why it taste so good!\n```\n\n### C-006\n- **Locator:** dietitian conversation about blood sugar\n- **Capture method:** YouTube auto-caption transcript\n\n```text\nSo what you're experiencing there is energy levels, but it's blood sugar fluctuations. So when you have lots of added sugar in your diet, you can get really big fluctuations in your blood sugar levels, which make you feel like you've got a lot of energy and then holy crap, you're crashing, and you have no energy. And then, what you typically do when you feel like you have no energy especially if you're not paying attention, is to gravitate towards something that will give you quick energy, which sugar.\n```\n\n## Evidence items\n\n### E-001\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"I had lost all self-control in the face of a free donut or plate of cookies. And I made justifications for my actions. Excuses like: 'well, I did go to the gym today.' 'Overall I eat pretty healthy.' 'It's not like I do this every day.'... Until I started to do it every day.\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-001\n- **Locator:** opening monologue\n- **Persona tags:** P-01 (justification-crafter / self-aware snacker)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 1; Bank 3\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a (lived experience)\n- **Use / limits:** supports the justification-inventory and the escalation arc; one self-report\n- **Situation:** daily snacking habit forming\n- **Emotion:** rationalizing / creeping loss of control\n- **Brief beliefs:** \"I can control it\"; \"I deserve a treat\"\n- **Style slots:** JUSTIFICATION_MENU; ESCAPE_ROUTE\n- **Safety relevance:** NONE\n- **Grade rationale:** one source-bounded lived-experience account\n- **Scope:** this video only\n- **Counterevidence:** no population-level inference\n- **Permitted inference:** this narrator reports a gradual loss of control masked by justifications\n- **Prohibited inference:** does not establish prevalence\n- **Testimonial qualification:** NOT_CANDIDATE (returns to moderation)\n\n### E-002\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"instinctively I went to my snack cabinet and I'll show you guys what I found. That's it. Organic rice cake. Nothing, nothing sweet there.\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-002\n- **Locator:** ~7 days in, late night\n- **Persona tags:** P-01 (justification-crafter); P-02 (conditioned-craver)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 3; Bank 9\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** illustrates the automaticity of the craving→cabinet loop\n- **Situation:** late-night craving, automatic cabinet check\n- **Emotion:** frustration / emptiness\n- **Style slots:** SENSORY_DIALECT; INVERSION\n- **Permitted inference:** conditioned behavior fires before conscious thought\n- **Prohibited inference:** not universal\n- **Testimonial qualification:** NOT_CANDIDATE\n\n### E-003\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"No longer did I have huge swings of high to low energy throughout the day... So you go back up, you feel good for a little while, and then you crash again.\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-003\n- **Locator:** ~3 weeks in\n- **Persona tags:** P-01; P-03 (energy-seeker)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 3; Bank 7\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** self-reported energy stabilization after quitting sugar; supports the inversion\n- **Situation:** post-sugar energy pattern\n- **Emotion:** relief / discovery\n- **Style slots:** INVERSION; EVIDENCE_LEDGER\n- **Permitted inference:** removing sugar stabilized energy\n- **Prohibited inference:** not controlled-study evidence\n- **Testimonial qualification:** NOT_CANDIDATE\n\n### E-004\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"I love sweets, I love dessert, big fan, and I don't want to just get rid of it completely, because I think in this life we need to actually enjoy things, but I want to bring it back in moderation.\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-004\n- **Locator:** closing reflections\n- **Persona tags:** P-01; P-04 (moderation-believer)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 1; Bank 5\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** the classic moderation escape route; \"life is for enjoying\" justification\n- **Situation:** post-challenge decision point\n- **Emotion:** hopeful / bargaining\n- **Style slots:** JUSTIFICATION_MENU; ESCAPE_ROUTE\n- **Permitted inference:** the moderation belief survives even a successful 30-day quit\n- **Prohibited inference:** not evidence that moderation works long-term\n- **Testimonial qualification:** NOT_CANDIDATE\n\n### E-005\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"My granola bar was like 30 grams of sugar and I'm like, that's why it taste so good!\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-005\n- **Locator:** label-reading discovery\n- **Persona tags:** P-01; P-05 (hidden-sugar-discoverer)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 8; Bank 9\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** the hidden-sugar revelation; \"healthy\" food as sugar vehicle\n- **Situation:** discovering hidden sugar\n- **Emotion:** surprise / wry recognition\n- **Style slots:** VILLAIN; SENSORY_DIALECT\n- **Permitted inference:** \"healthy\" packaged foods can be sugar delivery systems\n- **Prohibited inference:** not a systematic survey\n- **Testimonial qualification:** NOT_CANDIDATE\n````\n\n---\n\n### S-002 — YouTube: \"I Quit Sugar for 90 Days\" (SQkCUyNeiQw)\n\n````markdown\n# S-002 — YouTube: \"I Quit Sugar for 90 Days (it's changing my life)\"\n\n- **Source ID:** S-002\n- **URL:** https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SQkCUyNeiQw\n- **Title:** I Quit Sugar for 90 Days (it's changing my life)\n- **Source type:** community (YouTube personal-experiment video with auto-caption transcript)\n- **Retrieved (UTC):** 2026-07-28\n- **Access / license basis:** public YouTube video; short quotation basis\n- **Excerpt / redistribution basis:** minimal quotation retained\n- **Required attribution:** YouTube channel\n- **Retention / deletion sensitivity:** public listing\n- **Privacy / personal-data basis:** voluntarily published\n- **Disposition:** ACCEPTED\n\n## Minimum retained excerpt\n\n### C-001\n- **Locator:** ~6-7 days into challenge\n- **Capture method:** YouTube auto-caption transcript\n\n```text\nhowever pretty quickly things started going downhill I remember with day six and seven my energy levels absolutely crashed I mean I was so exhausted literally I couldn't even move and that's no exaggeration I don't really know what that was whether it was my body kind of reacting badly I don't know but looking back that was the only negative side effect I experienced and that only lasted for like one or two days I remember I was so tired that I missed the week of uploading videos on this YouTube channel and that almost never happens\n```\n\n### C-002\n- **Locator:** ~2 weeks in\n- **Capture method:** YouTube auto-caption transcript\n\n```text\nabout two weeks into cutting sugar this is where the magic kind of happened in the second week that's when I really started to feel the most improvements in my everyday life so I started to feel much more energized I stopped to experience those kind of massive drops in energy in the afternoon\n```\n\n### C-003\n- **Locator:** closing reflections\n- **Capture method:** YouTube auto-caption transcript\n\n```text\nI've learned that honestly I love sugar way too much I really do have a sweet tooth and so since cutting sugar completely... cutting Sugar by 80 okay okay that's a lot easier... I see this as a lifestyle not a short-term diet\n```\n\n### C-004\n- **Locator:** belly fat observation\n- **Capture method:** YouTube auto-caption transcript\n\n```text\nthe belly fat around my abs pretty much went in just 30 40 days which I was really surprised about and my strength at the gym was going up\n```\n\n## Evidence items\n\n### E-001\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"with day six and seven my energy levels absolutely crashed I mean I was so exhausted literally I couldn't even move and that's no exaggeration\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-001\n- **Locator:** ~6-7 days in\n- **Persona tags:** P-02 (conditioned-craver); P-06 (withdrawal-survivor)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 3; Bank 7\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** vivid withdrawal description; supports the \"it gets worse before better\" arc\n- **Situation:** days 6-7 of sugar cessation\n- **Emotion:** exhaustion / shock\n- **Style slots:** INVERSION; EVIDENCE_LEDGER\n- **Permitted inference:** acute withdrawal can include severe fatigue\n- **Prohibited inference:** not universal; individual variation\n- **Testimonial qualification:** NOT_CANDIDATE\n\n### E-002\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"in the second week that's when I really started to feel the most improvements in my everyday life so I started to feel much more energized I stopped to experience those kind of massive drops in energy in the afternoon\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-002\n- **Locator:** ~2 weeks in\n- **Persona tags:** P-02; P-03 (energy-seeker)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 3; Bank 10\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** the turning point; afternoon crash elimination\n- **Situation:** second week of no sugar\n- **Emotion:** relief / discovery\n- **Style slots:** FREEDOM_TEXTURE; INVERSION\n- **Permitted inference:** energy stabilization occurs around week 2\n- **Prohibited inference:** not a clinical finding\n- **Testimonial qualification:** PARTIAL (compromise to 80/20)\n\n### E-003\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"I've learned that honestly I love sugar way too much... cutting Sugar by 80 okay okay that's a lot easier\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-003\n- **Locator:** closing reflections\n- **Persona tags:** P-04 (moderation-believer)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 1; Bank 5\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** the moderation compromise; \"I love it too much to quit entirely\"\n- **Situation:** post-90-day decision\n- **Emotion:** self-awareness / bargaining\n- **Style slots:** JUSTIFICATION_MENU; ESCAPE_ROUTE\n- **Permitted inference:** moderation as the compromise after experiencing benefits\n- **Prohibited inference:** not evidence that 80/20 works forever\n- **Testimonial qualification:** NOT_CANDIDATE\n````\n\n---\n\n### S-003 — YouTube: \"I Quit Sugar for 30 Days! (Here's What Happened)\" — Anna Saccone\n\nThis is the richest personal narrative in the set.\n\n````markdown\n# S-003 — YouTube: \"I Quit Sugar for 30 Days! (Here's What Happened)\" — Anna Saccone\n\n- **Source ID:** S-003\n- **URL:** https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NOwb0-j7CAA\n- **Title:** I Quit Sugar for 30 Days! (Here's What Happened)\n- **Source type:** community (YouTube first-person narrative with transcript)\n- **Retrieved (UTC):** 2026-07-28\n- **Access / license basis:** public YouTube video; short quotation basis\n- **Excerpt / redistribution basis:** minimal quotation retained\n- **Required attribution:** Anna Saccone (channel owner)\n- **Retention / deletion sensitivity:** public listing\n- **Privacy / personal-data basis:** voluntarily published; name is public\n- **Disposition:** ACCEPTED\n\n## Minimum retained excerpt\n\n### C-001\n- **Locator:** opening ~1:00\n- **Capture method:** YouTube transcript\n\n```text\nI've always had a sweet tooth. You know, it's always been my thing. I loved sugar. I loved cupcakes. I loved cakes. I loved pastries, bread, donuts.\n```\n\n### C-002\n- **Locator:** describing the daily cycle\n- **Capture method:** YouTube transcript\n\n```text\nI used to eat healthy all day and then I'd always have a little sweet treat usually in the afternoon because that was when my blood sugar dipped. And I would get this very prominent low where I would almost feel well, I would feel quite lethargic and I always needed a pickme up with a coffee and a cake. I did not know that by feeding this habit, it was actually keeping the cycle alive. And so I would always do it like every single day. It was my sweet treat and my coffee. And I didn't realize how this was actually wreaking havoc with my blood sugar. And it was keeping me stuck in this constant tired, fatigued cycle.\n```\n\n### C-003\n- **Locator:** on the cravings disappearing\n- **Capture method:** YouTube transcript\n\n```text\nI loved sweets, like I said in the beginning of this video, and I used to watch other people on social media talk about how they quit sugar, how their cravings went away, and how their taste buds changed. And I honestly thought they were lying. I thought there's no way they're telling the truth. But I'm here to tell you that it is true. It is fact. I now walk into I would say after about a week or two, maybe two weeks, I could go into a bakery or any kind of a pastry shop or anything like that and honestly not want a thing. I honest I think if you fuel yourself properly and you don't let yourself get hungry, you just don't want those things anymore. And that's what happened to me.\n```\n\n### C-004\n- **Locator:** the almond croissant incident\n- **Capture method:** YouTube transcript\n\n```text\nI remember one time I went to meet my friend for a coffee and this was a few weeks in and I decided cuz like I said I wasn't super strict. Um and I decided to get a an almond croissant which was one of my favorite things to get. So I got it, didn't think anything of it and then I ate it and that whole rest of the day I felt terrible. My I had a tummy ache. It didn't even taste that great. It was so unbearably sweet to me. I just didn't even really enjoy it. And I am sitting here as a person who never thought I would say those words. Like never ever.\n```\n\n### C-005\n- **Locator:** benefits: skin, mood, sleep\n- **Capture method:** YouTube transcript\n\n```text\nclearer skin for sure. This has been a gamechanger for my skin. My skin has never looked better... I don't get the kinds of hormonal breakouts that I used to. I don't get those energy dips during the day as much. Like, I'm not kind of going like this all the time. My moods are better. I feel more in control of my mood.\n```\n\n### C-006\n- **Locator:** 6 months later, what's sustainable\n- **Capture method:** YouTube transcript\n\n```text\nI still generally stay away from processed sugar in general. I do not have a sweet treat every single day like what I used to, but I may have one maybe once a week, maybe once every two. It really varies because I don't crave it anymore... I had birthday cake one of the birthdays and then 10 days later I didn't. Or maybe it was the other way around. I can't remember. whichever one it was, I really didn't feel the need to have cake again 10 days later, you know, and that's something that I never could have said before. I would never pass up a slice of cake before, ever.\n```\n\n### C-007\n- **Locator:** the Christmas trigger\n- **Capture method:** YouTube transcript\n\n```text\nit was Christmas. I'm half Italian and at Christmas we always eat copious amounts of panaton, Pandorao, all those kinds of sweet treats and things. And we have them for breakfast. Like that's an Italian thing is that you eat sweets for breakfast, which now I've learned is such a bad habit... having that amount of sugar early in the morning was causing my blood sugar to be so out of whack. And I was just craving sugar all day every day.\n```\n\n### C-008\n- **Locator:** on weight\n- **Capture method:** YouTube transcript\n\n```text\nI did not lose a fraction of a pound by quitting sugar. So, in case you were wondering, cuz I know some of you were, how much weight did you lose? Absolutely zero.\n```\n\n## Evidence items\n\n### E-001\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"I used to watch other people on social media talk about how they quit sugar, how their cravings went away, and how their taste buds changed. And I honestly thought they were lying. I thought there's no way they're telling the truth. But I'm here to tell you that it is true. It is fact.\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-003\n- **Locator:** cravings/taste bud section\n- **Persona tags:** P-01; P-03; P-07 (skeptical-sweet-tooth)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 2; Bank 3; Bank 10\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** core belief reversal — from \"they're lying\" to \"it's true\"; powerful for the skeptical reader\n- **Situation:** after experiencing craving disappearance\n- **Emotion:** astonishment / conversion\n- **Style slots:** BELIEF_MAP; MOMENT_OF_REVELATION; FREEDOM_TESTIMONY\n- **Permitted inference:** craving extinction can feel unbelievable until experienced\n- **Prohibited inference:** not universal guarantee\n- **Testimonial qualification:** STRONG_CANDIDATE\n\n### E-002\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"I did not know that by feeding this habit, it was actually keeping the cycle alive... It was keeping me stuck in this constant tired, fatigued cycle.\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-002\n- **Locator:** daily cycle description\n- **Persona tags:** P-03 (energy-seeker); P-08 (cycle-trapped)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 2; Bank 7\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** perfect inversion articulation — the \"fix\" is the cause\n- **Situation:** daily afternoon crash → treat ritual\n- **Emotion:** realization / regret\n- **Style slots:** INVERSION; KEYSTONE_BELIEF\n- **Permitted inference:** the sugar that \"fixes\" the crash caused it\n- **Prohibited inference:** not mechanistic proof\n- **Testimonial qualification:** STRONG_CANDIDATE\n\n### E-003\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"I ate it and that whole rest of the day I felt terrible. My I had a tummy ache. It didn't even taste that great. It was so unbearably sweet to me. I just didn't even really enjoy it. And I am sitting here as a person who never thought I would say those words. Like never ever.\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-004\n- **Locator:** almond croissant incident\n- **Persona tags:** P-07 (skeptical-sweet-tooth); P-09 (taste-bud-reset)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 3; Bank 4; Bank 10\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** the \"favorite food becomes disgusting\" moment — a freedom proof point\n- **Situation:** eating a former favorite pastry weeks into quitting\n- **Emotion:** disgust / disbelief\n- **Style slots:** SPECIAL_MOMENTS; SENSORY_DIALECT; FREEDOM_TESTIMONY\n- **Permitted inference:** taste recalibration can make former pleasures repellent\n- **Prohibited inference:** individual variation expected\n- **Testimonial qualification:** STRONG_CANDIDATE\n\n### E-004\n- **Kind:** EXACT_QUOTE\n- **Text:** \"I would never pass up a slice of cake before, ever. So, that's very different to me.\"\n- **Excerpt ID:** C-006\n- **Locator:** 6 months later\n- **Persona tags:** P-07; P-10 (identity-shifted)\n- **Bank slots:** Bank 10; Bank 2\n- **Evidence grade:** n/a\n- **Use / limits:** identity change — \"that's not who I am anymore\"\n- **Situation:** second birthday in 10 days, didn't want cake\n- **Emotion:** surprise / pride\n- **Style slots:*