uck in this constant tired, fatigued cycle. Every single time of the month, I would get these hormonal breakouts.\nAnd this is something that I'd been dealing with since I was a teenager. And I felt like 37 was kind of too old for this now. Like really, can I not have a break? So that was mega frustrating. I had tried giving up dairy in the past. I tried all these different things. None of them worked because I was still hooked on sugar. I just want to reiterate that this whole journey with giving up sugar has never been about restriction for me. It's always come from a place of healing and just wanting to feel better. So what led to me finally making the decision to quit sugar? So, it was Christmas. I'm half Italian and at Christmas we always eat copious amounts of panaton, Pandorao, all those kinds of sweet treats and things. And we have them for breakfast.\nLike that's an Italian thing is that you eat sweets for breakfast, which now I've learned is such a bad habit. But anyway, I've been on that train like I have my whole life at Christmas. You know, you're indulging a bit more. But what I was really noticing is having that amount of sugar early in the morning was causing my blood sugar to be so out of whack. And I was just craving sugar all day every day. Compared to when I usually start the day with a more savory breakfast or like a more protein heavy breakfast, I didn't get these kinds of cravings. The proof was in the pudding literally because I was able to decipher between the two different days. Like if I had a healthy breakfast, which was normally year round, versus at Christmas when I was having panaton or Pandorao for breakfast, I was just feeling honestly really really crappy all day long just because of that breakfast choice. So I decided I think it was like the 27th of December or something. I was like, you know what? I'm going to try this quitting sugar thing. I'm I was so by that stage I was so sick of it too that I just wanted to try it. I did not 