 best friend. I did protein shakes, Greek yogurt with berries, and some almond butter or peanut butter saved my life so many times. Basically, anywhere you can get protein or fat, you're winning because those things will keep you full and satisfied and it will keep you from feeling those sweet cravings because eventually they go away. I know it sounds insane and trust me, you are talking to or you're listening to a girl who had the sweetest tooth known to man.\nI loved sweets, like I said in the beginning of this video, and I used to watch other people on social media talk about how they quit sugar, how their cravings went away, and how their taste buds changed. And I honestly thought they were lying. I thought there's no way they're telling the truth. But I'm here to tell you that it is true. It is fact. I now walk into I would say after about a week or two, maybe two weeks, I could go into a bakery or any kind of a pastry shop or anything like that and honestly not want a thing. I honest I think if you fuel yourself properly and you don't let yourself get hungry, you just don't want those things anymore. And that's what happened to me.\nI'm not saying that this will happen to everybody, but from what I've seen and from what I've heard, the stories are pretty similar. And I do think that naturally your taste buds do change and you start to have a more savory tooth than a sweet tooth. And I never thought that would happen to me, but it did.\nAlso, things that you used to really enjoy that were very sweet will taste kind of sickly sweet to you. Like I remember one time I went to meet my friend for a coffee and this was a few weeks in and I decided cuz like I said I wasn't super strict. Um and I decided to get a an almond croissant which was one of my favorite things to get. So I got it, didn't think anything of it and then I ate it and that whole rest of the day I felt terrible. My I had a tummy ache. It didn't even taste that great. It was so unbearably sweet to me. I just didn't even really enjoy it. And I am sitting here as a person who never thought I would say those words. Like never ever. Let's talk about the benefits that happened. Okay, so first thing, clearer skin for sure. This has been a gamecher for my skin. My skin has never looked better. I feel like whether it's the products I'm using, the lifestyle, hydration, the lack of sugar, I feel like it all works together, sleep probably too, but definitely quitting sugar, I did notice huge difference in my skin. I don't get the kinds of hormonal breakouts that I used to. I don't get those energy dips during the day as much. Like, I'm not kind of going like this all the time. My moods are better. I feel more in control of my mood. I won't lie, I do always go a little bit insane coming up to that time of the month, but I'm not as bad as what I used to be when I was still consuming sugar. Let's just put it that way.\nSleep. Okay, so I've got a cold right now. So, I didn't sleep very well last night, but that is rare. I generally will go to sleep and sleep the whole night through from 9:00 until 500 a.m.\nThat's my bedtime. That's my That's my routine. And with quitting sugar, provided I don't drink too much water close to bedtime, I sleep the whole eight hours and I sleep really, really well. Here's where I'm at 6 months later and what has become sustainable for me.\nSo, I still generally stay away from processed sugar in general. I do not have a sweet treat every single day like what I used to, but I may have one maybe once a week, maybe once every two. It really varies because I don't crave it anymore. It really varies. Obviously, if I'm going to, you know, a birthday party or if it's a friend's birthday or family's birthday, I will, of course, I won't say no to a slice of cake, but only if I really, really want it. And that's been key. Like, we have two birthdays really close together in our family. I had birthday cake one of the birthdays and then 10 days later I didn't. Or maybe it was the other way around. I can't remember. whichever one it was, I really didn't feel the need to have cake again 10 days later, you know, and that's something that I never could have said before. I would never pass up a slice of cake before, ever. So, that's very different to me in terms of how I approach this as a mom raising a family with kids and all of that. I am still very balanced as a mom. Like, I don't restrict anything really within reason.\nOf course, I encourage my kids to eat healthy snacks, but I also will never say no to, you know, if I bring them out to a cafe, they can have cake, they can have a muffin. They're kids at the end of the day. I ate sugar when I was a child, and so did all my siblings. I feel like the world just doesn't work in black and white. And that way of thinking is just never really healthy.\nAnd I think people can get too bogged down with it all. Like, I even see it in the comments sometimes. People are like, \"I can't believe that you buy mayonnaise or I can't believe that you buy white bread.\" And I'm like, I get it. You know, obviously we learn in nutrition school that these things are not ideal, but also there's gray areas when you're raising kids. When you're raising a family, there's certain tastes, there's certain things that they will and won't accept, will and won't like. I personally feel like if their three main meals are healthy, then I'm doing a good job. And I'm definitely not that mom that's super strict or restrictive because I don't think that that really solves anything, especially when kids are young. Again, for me, this is my personal thing. It was my personal journey and I had to be in the right place to fully go there. And not everybody is ready, and that's okay. You might be watching this video and thinking, there's no way I'm giving up sugar. That's absolutely fine. I'm not here to change anyone's mind. Trust me, I have been there. I've been at every different stage and every stage is valid